          "derivation": "The word is real: Skt. laktaka- 'a dirty and tattered cloth, a rag' (Monier-Williams; Apte 'lac; a tattered cloth, a rag'; Shabda-Sagara 'old and tattered cloth, a rag or rags; lac, the dye'). What is wrong is the arrow: the chart derives it from Greek λακίς (parent #5). That is impossible — Classical Sanskrit borrows nothing of this kind from Greek (the handful of Greek loans are technical, astronomical and late, e.g. horā, kendra), and a Greek stem lakid- could not surface as laktaka- with an unexplained -t-. Nourai's own source does not say it either: Mann (IEC 661) lists 'Skt. (dim. type) laktakah \"rag\"' as a sister member of the t-stem *lakt-, alongside Per. laxt and Arm. latʿ, not as a Greek derivative. And the standard handbooks reject the equation altogether: Walde-Pokorny II 420 says 'Ai. laktaka- m. \"Lappen\" (Prellwitz² 258) widerspricht im Ablaut' — the ablaut grade does not fit — and Pokorny 674 lists no Indic reflex at all. The Indian grammatical tradition analyses laktaka- as lakta- + -ka, tied to lākṣā 'lac dye' (the cloth sense arising from the red-dyed rag). So the node should at best be an independent branch off the root, and even that is disputed.",
