          "derivation": "Gk. λαπαρός 'slack, loose (of the bowels), soft, hollow', with the substantive λαπάρα 'the flank, loins' and the verb λαπάσσω 'to empty, evacuate'. Watkins puts it exactly where Nourai does: 'Suffixed variant form *lap-aro- in Greek laparos, soft: LAPAROTOMY. [Pok. 2. lep- 678]', and Pokorny 678 includes it under lep-2 as '*λεπαρός', i.e. an a-vocalism variant of the 'peel → thin, fine' family (cf. λεπτός). But this is not the only view: Walde-Pokorny II 429 explicitly refers λαπαρός away — 'über λαπαρός \"schmächtig, dünn\" s. aber u. *lap- \"klein\"' — to a separate root, and current Greek etymology (Beekes, followed by en.wiktionary) groups λαπαρός with λαγαρός, πλαδαρός, χαλαρός and, because of the prothetic vowel of ἀλαπάζω, suspects Pre-Greek substrate origin. So the node is right in form and meaning but the attachment to *lep- is one of at least three competing accounts.",
