          "derivation": "A 19th-century medical coinage, not an inherited word: Klein s.v. laparotomy — 'Coined by the English surgeon Sir Thomas Bryant (1828-1914) fr. laparo- and Gk. -τομία, \"cutting of\", fr. -τομή, \"a cutting, section\"', where laparo- (before a vowel lapar-) is the combining form of Gk. λαπάρα 'the flank, loins' (so also laparectomy, laparoscopy). The gloss on the chart, 'surgical incision into abdominal wall', is exactly the technical sense, and the semantic step is Greek 'flank, soft part of the body' → the anatomical 'abdominal wall'. Watkins lists LAPAROTOMY under lep-1, i.e. the same chain the chart draws (root → Greek laparos → English).",
